{# DECIDED: the sentinel admits pods by the `netpol-probe: control` label, not by an
   `app:` label, and its name differs from the app it selects, so it stays bespoke.
   The callers model (`netpol_from` / `netpol_fences`, macro in
   k8s/templates/networkpolicy.yml.j2) renders one TCP port admitting same-namespace `app:`
   peers, and its header rules out growing it to absorb another shape.
#}
---
# The probes' POSITIVE CONTROL, and the reason it is a policy rather than an accident.
#
# Every probe job needs one leg that MUST succeed. Without it, an inverted leg ("this connection
# must be refused") passes for the wrong reason whenever the probe pod has no network, DNS is
# down, or the target is simply dead. No pod in this namespace is unfenced under
# `podSelector: {}`, so the control target is named here. That is STRICTLY STRONGER than
# reaching an unfenced pod, which proves only that the probe has a network: reaching a FENCED
# pod that admits us proves the admit path itself still works, which distinguishes "denied by
# policy" from "the policy engine is dropping everything".
#
# speedtest, deliberately, and NOT littlelink: littlelink is the verify Job's DENIED target, so
# admitting the probes there would make that probe report POLICY NOT ENFORCED. speedtest is a
# static bandwidth page, is no probe's target, and has no data and no dependents.
#
# Adding a probe? Give its pod `netpol-probe: control` and dial `speedtest:80`.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: netpol-sentinel
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: speedtest
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# Renders allow-all when the flag is false, so `netpol_baseline_enforced` is a real rollback
   lever. `| bool` for the string trap. Both explained in full in
   networkpolicy-traefik.yaml.j2. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_enforced | bool %}
    # speedtest's real callers (traefik, prometheus) come from the baseline policy, which already
    # selects it. This rule adds the probe pods and nothing else. Port 80 is the containerPort,
    # which for speedtest equals the Service port.
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              netpol-probe: control
      ports:
        - port: 80
          protocol: TCP
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
